From 39090bd6fdab0917d0b9c60a526491b87229b627 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Andrey Bodrov <ap.bodrov@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 19 Aug 2026 00:40:40 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] linuxkpi: dma_alloc_coherent returned CACHEABLE memory on
 arm64

kmem_alloc_contig(..., VM_MEMATTR_DEFAULT) is WRITE_BACK on arm64, so the
allocator whose entire contract is "no cache maintenance required" handed out
cacheable memory and then gave its physical address to a device. On an
architecture without coherent DMA that is exactly backwards: the CPU stores sit
in cache, the device reads DRAM and sees stale contents, and nobody flushes
anything because callers of this API are entitled to assume they need not. x86
never notices, its DMA being coherent by architecture.

Diagnosed rather than guessed. lima allocates its Mali GPU page tables through
this path, and the GPU MMU faulted on an address whose PTE the CPU could read back
perfectly well:

    mmu page fault at 0x286000 ... of type read on gpmmu
      -> PTE = 0x649371df: the page IS mapped

That verdict comes from a probe added to lima s fault handler for this purpose,
walking the live tables with the same macros as the mapping path. The mapping
code, the TLB zap implementation and both of its call sites were each verified
byte-identical to upstream Linux BEFORE reaching for a coherency explanation --
the tables were correct and simply not visible to the device.

Now VM_MEMATTR_UNCACHEABLE on aarch64/arm/riscv, which is what
bus_dmamem_alloc(BUS_DMA_COHERENT) does in the same situation, and unchanged
elsewhere so no platform gives up write-back caching for nothing.

RESULT: first frame. Mali-400 renders, 5/5 runs, zero GPU MMU faults --
  PASS stage A -- the PP executed a job
  PASS stage B -- GP built a tile list and PP rasterised it
  centre pixel = 0 255 0 255
---
 sys/compat/linuxkpi/common/src/linux_pci.c | 37 +++++++++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 36 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/sys/compat/linuxkpi/common/src/linux_pci.c b/sys/compat/linuxkpi/common/src/linux_pci.c
index fc8666c76..4c2facf3e 100644
--- a/sys/compat/linuxkpi/common/src/linux_pci.c
+++ b/sys/compat/linuxkpi/common/src/linux_pci.c
@@ -249,6 +249,20 @@ linux_pdev_dma_init(struct pci_dev *pdev)
  */
 #define	LINUX_DMA_TAG_NSEGMENTS	1024
 
+/*
+ * Memory attribute for dma_alloc_coherent(). "Coherent" is a promise to the
+ * caller that no cache maintenance is required, so on an architecture without
+ * coherent DMA it has to be uncacheable memory -- which is exactly what
+ * bus_dmamem_alloc(BUS_DMA_COHERENT) does for the same situation. Left as
+ * VM_MEMATTR_DEFAULT where DMA really is coherent, so nothing gives up write-back
+ * caching for no reason.
+ */
+#if defined(__aarch64__) || defined(__arm__) || defined(__riscv)
+#define	LKPI_DMA_COHERENT_MEMATTR	VM_MEMATTR_UNCACHEABLE
+#else
+#define	LKPI_DMA_COHERENT_MEMATTR	VM_MEMATTR_DEFAULT
+#endif
+
 int
 linux_dma_tag_init(struct device *dev, u64 dma_mask)
 {
@@ -1820,8 +1834,29 @@ linux_dma_alloc_coherent(struct device *dev, size_t size,
 	align = PAGE_SIZE << get_order(size);
 	/* Always zero the allocation. */
 	flag |= M_ZERO;
+	/*
+	 * VM_MEMATTR_DEFAULT is WRITE_BACK on arm64, which makes this allocator
+	 * return CACHEABLE memory -- and then hands the physical address to a
+	 * device. On an architecture whose DMA is not coherent that defeats the
+	 * entire point of dma_alloc_coherent(): the CPU's stores sit in its cache,
+	 * the device reads DRAM and sees stale contents, and no cache maintenance
+	 * happens anywhere because callers of this API are entitled to assume none
+	 * is needed. x86 never notices because its DMA is coherent by
+	 * architecture.
+	 *
+	 * Measured on a Mali-400 (lima) via drm-kmod, aarch64: lima allocates its
+	 * GPU page tables here, and the Mali MMU page-faulted on an address whose
+	 * PTE the CPU could read back perfectly well --
+	 *
+	 *   mmu page fault at 0x286000 ... of type read on gpmmu
+	 *     -> PTE = 0x649371df: the page IS mapped
+	 *
+	 * The mapping code, the TLB zap and its call sites were all verified
+	 * identical to upstream Linux first; the tables were simply never visible
+	 * to the device.
+	 */
 	mem = kmem_alloc_contig(size, flag & GFP_NATIVE_MASK, 0, high,
-	    align, 0, VM_MEMATTR_DEFAULT);
+	    align, 0, LKPI_DMA_COHERENT_MEMATTR);
 	if (mem != NULL) {
 		*dma_handle = linux_dma_map_phys_common(dev, vtophys(mem), size,
 		    priv->dmat_coherent);
-- 
2.55.0

